  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23686214
    description: "Version 0.0.1"
abstract: >-
  Transfers a concept abstraction to a new visual domain with the rule held fixed, and
  measures how many labelled target examples re-grounding the concepts takes. A
  convolutional network learns three concepts of synthetic scenes, round, tone and left,
  on a source style; a target style draws them with other colours, sizes and blur, and the
  rule over them, their conjunction, is never trained. Over 20 seeds of 1000 test scenes,
  re-grounding the network's head from concept labels brings tone to 90 % in all 20 seeds,
  with a lower median of 16 target scenes; left carries over, and round does not re-ground
  within 64. Trained from verdicts alone, the model gets 99.8 % of the source verdicts
  right through outputs that do not mean the named concepts, a reasoning shortcut. Target
  verdicts passed through the fixed rule rank target scenes with 12.5 % (6.2 to 25.0)
  false positives at 90 % sensitivity at 64 scenes, against 58.8 % (18.9 to 88.8) for a
  network with no concepts and no rule, yet do not re-ground the concepts. Every published
  count is reproduced by a committed command, and CI reruns the first seed of each study
  on GitHub's AMD EPYC and Intel Xeon runners.
repository-code: "https://github.com/ppigazzini/concept-regrounding"
keywords:
  - abstraction
  - concept learning
  - domain shift
